1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Vikings saw — a wolf’s jaw.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Scandinavians still call Christmas jul.
The old English word is Yule.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Before Christianity reached the North,
the feast ran three nights from
midwinter.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
A late-1100s Icelandic manuscript names
this V of stars Úlfs kjaptr: the wolf’s
jaw.

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Which wolf? The book doesn’t say. But in
Norse myth, the sky is always on the
run.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The jaw opens beside the road of the Sun
and Moon. So some guess it waits to bite
them.

7
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Babylon: a bull’s jaw. The Tikuna: a
crocodile’s mouth. The Tupi: a tapir’s
jaw. But the Inuit saw dogs.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Late December: the wolf’s jaw climbs in
the east, with Orion’s Belt below it.

9
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Inuit: telling time in the polar night.

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

